# Line-ending policy.
#
# All text is stored with LF endings in the repository, and checked out with LF
# on every platform, including Windows.
#
# The `eol=lf` rules matter for correctness, not just tidiness:
#
#   * Protobuf codegen. buf's comment normalisation differs between CRLF and LF
#     input. For the vendored CJK comment in common/constant/NotifyMsgType.proto
#     it emits "//行情..." from a CRLF input and "// 行情..." from an LF input, so
#     `make proto-verify` reported drift that depended on the contributor's
#     platform. Pinning the input to LF makes generation byte-identical
#     everywhere.
#
#   * Local gofmt. On a CRLF checkout `gofmt -l .` lists every file, because
#     gofmt normalises to LF. That made a plain gofmt check useless on Windows
#     while CI, which sees LF, was authoritative. With LF checkouts the local
#     result matches CI and `make fmt-check` is no longer needed to emulate it.
#
# proto/ is vendored byte-for-byte from the vendor SDK (see proto/PROVENANCE.md).
# Storing LF does not re-encode or reformat it; the committed bytes are the
# normalised form git already produced.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.go text eol=lf
*.proto text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
